# ALSA configuration for running AAE under WSL. Copy to ~/.asoundrc:
#
#   cp scripts/linux/wsl-asoundrc ~/.asoundrc
#
# WHY THIS IS NEEDED
#
# /dev/snd under WSL contains only 'timer' - there is no PCM device at all - so
# the stock "default" definition in /usr/share/alsa/pcm/default.conf resolves to
# a sound card that does not exist and snd_pcm_open("default") fails with
# ENOENT. alsa_backend.cpp reports that correctly and AAE runs silently.
#
# Installing libasound2-plugins is necessary but NOT sufficient. It supplies the
# pulse plugin and /usr/share/alsa/alsa.conf.d/50-pulseaudio.conf, but that file
# only defines a NAMED pcm.pulse - its own first line says "typically useful for
# testing". The drop-in that redirects the DEFAULT PCM to PulseAudio ships with
# the pulseaudio SERVER package, which is not installed under WSL because WSLg
# provides the server externally over /mnt/wslg/PulseServer. So nothing ever
# points "default" at pulse, and that is what this file does.
#
# Nothing here applies to SteamOS or a Pi, where a real PCM device exists and
# "default" resolves on its own. This is WSL-only setup.

pcm.!default {
    type pulse
}

ctl.!default {
    type pulse
}
